---
/**
 * CONTACT — the one place a person writes to us, whichever kind of writing it is.
 *
 * It used to be `/submit`, reached from a sidebar entry called `Get in touch` and from a second
 * page called `Tickets` whose only real content was a button pointing here. Two doors into one
 * room, both buried under forty pages of documentation. The sweep of twelve portals
 * (`research-portal-ia-2026-08-19.md`) says the same thing every time: contact is a HEADER
 * destination, and "open one" and "see the ones I opened" are different things — Zapier is the only
 * portal in the sweep with a public ticket system and it carries `Contact Support` and
 * `My Requests` as two separate items. So this page is the form, and seeing your history is a
 * sign-in, stated once at the foot rather than given a page of its own.
 *
 * Five doors, two destinations — and the fork is the safeguard, not a convenience.
 *
 * Dan asked for two things that share a shape: anonymous ticket submission (name, email, subject,
 * body) and public feature requests. They must NOT share a destination. A bug report carries
 * someone's data — a base name, a record, sometimes a screenshot with customer rows in it — and
 * publishing one to a voting board is a privacy incident, not a UX slip. So the first question is
 * which kind it is, and the public path says out loud that it will be visible before anyone types.
 *
 * FOUR OF THE FIVE ARE PRIVATE, and only the public one warns. `billing` and `other` arrived on
 * 2026-08-20: an invoice question was landing in the form headed "Something is broken", which
 * routes an account question to the people who read bug reports and tells the person writing it
 * that they have a fault. Both are private, so both are built from the same block as the ticket —
 * which is why the three are one array below rather than three near-identical copies of the same
 * markup. Three copies is how the fourth field gets added to two of them.
 *
 * `sales` IS PRIVATE AND IS STILL HAND-WRITTEN, and the reason is the same test the `PRIVATE`
 * array was built to pass. That array exists to hold ONE attachment block, ONE email hint and ONE
 * private note, because those are the parts that drift when copied. The sales fork shares none of
 * them: it has no attachment block at all, its email cannot say "we use it to find your account"
 * to someone who has not got one, and its note names a desk that is not support. Folding it in
 * would mean wrapping the attachment block in a flag, the email hint in a second, and the platform
 * field in a third — a config object with a clause per member, which is the copies wearing a hat.
 * What it does share with every other door is the machinery: one `KINDS` list, one URL guard, one
 * step machine, one validator.
 *
 * ATTACHMENTS ARE ON THE THREE PRIVATE FORMS AND NOT ON THE PUBLIC ONE. Dan asked for screenshots
 * (2026-08-20), and a screenshot is the single fastest way to publish something you did not mean
 * to: dragged into a post that lands on a public board, a base name, a customer's row and an email
 * address go with it. The same argument that forked this page in the first place forbids the file
 * input on the request form, and the request form is the one place a person is least likely to
 * need one.
 *
 * DUPLICATE DETECTION IS THE HIGH-VALUE PART. Every public board drowns in forty phrasings of one
 * idea, and the sweep found nobody solving it at submission time — they all merge by hand
 * afterwards. We can solve it before it happens with machinery already here: each request has its
 * own page (`/roadmap/[slug]`), so Pagefind indexes it, so `searchDocs` finds it. Someone who sees
 * their idea already there votes instead of posting, which is what everyone wants and nobody builds.
 *
 * THE FORK IS THE DEFAULT, NOT A TOLL. Five links lead here and four of them already know the
 * answer — three say "Add a request" and one says "Report a problem". Asking those people to choose
 * again is asking them to repeat the click they just made. All five kinds skip straight to their
 * form through `?kind=`, which still says which one it is and still offers the way back.
 *
 * NOTHING IS SENT. There is no backend in this repo. Every path ends in a confirmation that says
 * exactly that rather than pretending — the handoff at the foot lists what the real one must do.
 * The attachment block stops at the same line: files are collected, listed and counted, and the
 * form never leaves the page.
 */
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import RelatedToField from '../components/RelatedToField.astro';
import { ATTACH_ACCEPT, ATTACH_HINT } from '../lib/submit';
import { spacesByOrg } from '../data/spaces';

const APP_LOGIN_URL = 'https://app.baseout.com/login?returnTo=https://support.baseout.com/contact';

/**
 * The Spaces the picker offers, grouped by organisation.
 *
 * DERIVED IN THE FRONTMATTER, because it is the same list for every door that shows it and a
 * component that regrouped the roster per render would be doing one job three times. Empty is a
 * REAL STATE and it is handled by the template rather than by an empty control: a person whose
 * account holds no Space at all would otherwise be shown a picker whose only line is "not about a
 * particular Space", which is a question with no answers on it.
 */
const SPACE_GROUPS = spacesByOrg();

/**
 * WHAT A SIGNED-OUT PERSON DOES WITH THE SAME KNOWLEDGE, and the whole ruling in one sentence.
 *
 * A signed-out visitor may well know exactly which Space they mean — they are usually a colleague
 * of the account holder — but we do not know who they are, so there is no roster to offer them and
 * no way to check an answer they type. THREE OPTIONS WERE ON THE TABLE and two were rejected:
 *
 *   A free-text box is honest to the person and dishonest to the data. `Ticket.spaceId` is an id
 *     that a real store JOINS; filling it with "the finance one, I think" makes every consumer of
 *     that column — the chip on the request list, any routing rule, any report — read a string that
 *     resolves to nothing. Keeping it honest would mean a SECOND column carrying an unjoinable
 *     hint, which is a schema change to hold a sentence.
 *   Hiding the question entirely is simpler and loses exactly what the person knows, which is the
 *     one thing this change exists to capture.
 *
 * So the field is signed-in only and the KNOWLEDGE still gets asked for — in the body hint, which is
 * already the place this form collects the facts it cannot structure ("Run IDs, base names and error
 * text all help"). The Space joins that list for the reader who has no picker. It costs no new
 * field, no unjoinable column, and it lands in the one place a human triager reads anyway.
 *
 * ONE SENTENCE FOR BOTH DOORS. It is the same request whether the body asked "What happened?" or
 * "What is it?", and two near-identical strings is how one of them goes stale.
 */
const SPACE_PROSE_OUT = 'If you know which Space it is about, name it here too.';

/**
 * The three private forms, written once.
 *
 * They differ in four strings and nothing else, and the thing that must never differ is the part
 * nobody looks at twice: the private note, the email hint, the attachment block. Copy-pasted, this
 * page would carry three attachment blocks that start identical and end up answering to three
 * different sets of rules. The public request form stays hand-written below — it has different
 * fields, live duplicate detection and no file input, so folding it in here would mean a config
 * object with a flag for every clause, which is the same three copies wearing a hat.
 *
 * `space` IS THE FOURTH FIELD THIS ARRAY WAS BUILT TO SURVIVE, and it goes on two of the three.
 * A fault and a catch-all are both about something INSIDE the product, and the Space is the object
 * that routes them: `data/tickets.ts` has carried `spaceId` since the first case and nothing has
 * ever filled it, so the first reply on a failed backup is "which Space was this?". Billing is the
 * exception and it is an argued one — an invoice belongs to the ACCOUNT, not to a Space, so on that
 * door the honest answer is "not about a particular Space" every single time, and a control whose
 * resting state is always the answer is furniture in front of someone trying to sort out a payment.
 * `sales` is excluded by its premise (no account, therefore no Spaces) and `request` by the fork's
 * own rule (a Space name is account data, and the public board is where account data must not go).
 */
const PRIVATE = [
  {
    kind: 'ticket',
    space: true,
    heading: 'Something is broken',
    note: 'This goes privately to support. Nothing here appears on the public board.',
    bodyLabel: 'What happened?',
    bodyHint:
      'Run IDs, base names and error text all help. Please leave out anything you would not want support to read.',
    filesHint: 'A screenshot of what you saw, the run that failed, or the error message.',
    submit: 'Send to support',
  },
  {
    kind: 'billing',
    space: false,
    heading: 'Account and billing',
    note: 'This goes privately to the people who hold your account. Nothing here appears on the public board.',
    bodyLabel: 'What do you need?',
    /* NAMED SO NOBODY SENDS A CARD NUMBER. People paste one when a payment fails and the field says
       nothing, and once it is in a ticket it is in a mailbox, a database and a backup. Saying which
       four digits are enough is what stops it, and it has to be said before the box, not after. */
    bodyHint:
      'An invoice number, the plan you are on, or the last four digits of the card if a payment failed. Never send a full card number.',
    filesHint: 'The invoice or receipt in question, or a screenshot of the charge.',
    submit: 'Send to billing',
  },
  {
    kind: 'other',
    space: true,
    heading: 'Something else',
    note: 'This goes privately to support. Nothing here appears on the public board.',
    bodyLabel: 'What is it?',
    bodyHint:
      'Say as much as you can. If it turns out to be a fault or an idea, we will move it to the right place for you.',
    filesHint: 'A screenshot of the screen you are describing, if there is one.',
    submit: 'Send to support',
  },
];
---

{/* PRE-PAINT, AND DUPLICATED ON PURPOSE. `portal-session.ts` holds the same three lines and is the
    authority after this runs; a module cannot execute before the document is parsed, so a page that
    paints "Sign in" at a reader who is signed in has already said the wrong thing. Kept to one
    expression so there is nothing here to drift. */}
<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    var v = new URLSearchParams(location.search).get('session');
    document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-portal-session', v === 'out' ? 'out' : 'in');
  })();
</script>

<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    /* NEUTRAL, AND THE STEP SHARPENS IT. This page holds four kinds of writing, and it was titled
       `Report a problem` for both of the first two: a person who came to suggest a feature read a
       heading telling them they had a fault, above a form headed `Something I wish existed` (Oleh,
       2026-08-19). The server cannot know which fork a `?kind=` deep link asked for, so the static
       title is the one that is true of every step, and `lib/submit.ts` sharpens it to the chosen
       one.
       `Contact us`, not `Contact support` (Dan, 2026-08-20). Half these doors do not go to
       support. This string and `TITLES.fork` in `lib/submit.ts` must stay identical — this one is
       what renders, and the script overwrites it on the first step change. */
    title: 'Contact us',
    /* IT ENUMERATES THE DOORS, so it has to hold all of them. This is also the meta description,
       and the sales door is the one whose reader is most likely to arrive from a search engine
       rather than from inside the product. */
    description:
      'Report something broken, ask for something new, get help with your account, or ask about pricing before you start.',
    tableOfContents: false,
    /* NO PAGINATION. Starlight derives prev/next from the SIDEBAR, and this page is in the sidebar
       so a phone can reach it — which put it into the documentation's reading order. Measured: the
       docs home said "Previous: Contact". These two lines are what keep a service out of a
       sequence it is not part of; anything else added as a top-level sidebar link needs them too. */
    prev: false,
    next: false,
  }}
>
  <div class="sb" data-submit>
    {/* ── Step 1 · the fork ── */}
    <section class="sb-step" data-step="fork">
      {/* DEFLECTION BELONGS TO THE FORK, not to the page. Every portal in the sweep puts a "try this
          before you write" line ahead of the form — Notion, Figma and Grammarly all do it — but it
          only works while the visitor is still choosing. Left at page level it also showed above the
          two form steps, where someone has already picked a lane and is typing, and "go and read the
          documentation instead" arrives there as a brush-off rather than a shortcut. Moving it
          inside the fork also leaves Back as the first thing in every form step, which is where a
          way out belongs. Both cheaper routes are named because they answer different shapes of
          question: the documentation if you know roughly what you are looking for, the assistant if
          you only know what went wrong. Neither is a wall — the two cards are directly underneath. */}
      <p class="sb-deflect">
        <Icon name="sparkles" size={16} />
        <span>
          Most of this is already written down. <a href="/" data-search>Search the
          documentation</a>, or <a href="/" data-ask>ask the assistant</a> — it reads the same
          pages and answers in a sentence.
        </span>
      </p>

      <p class="sb-lede">What happens next depends on which of these it is, so it is worth asking
        first.</p>

      {/* TWO ROWS STILL, AND STILL NOTHING TO DECODE. Four tiles in a 2x2 was an explicit
          instruction (Oleh, 2026-08-20), replacing three shapes on the argument that different
          shapes make the reader work out a hierarchy before they can answer a question they
          already know the answer to. A fifth door cannot keep that grid: two columns give three
          rows with an orphan cell, and three columns give two rows with a hole that reads as a
          card which failed to load.

          What it keeps is the thing the instruction was protecting. The five doors split two and
          three along a line that is already drawn on this page in colour: the two LANES a person
          might be in (a fault, a wish) carry a tint, and the three DESKS they might want carry the
          neutral one. So the rows are the tints, and the two shapes are one fact stated twice
          rather than a second hierarchy to learn. Six columns underneath: three each on the top
          row, two each on the bottom, which leaves the two common doors at exactly the 344px they
          measure today and lands the grid flush on both rows with no empty cell anywhere.

          Frequency agrees with it. A broken thing and a feature request are the common cases and
          they are the large pair; billing, sales and the catch-all are the rare ones and they are
          the small three. Position already ranked them top-left to bottom-right, so size is
          carrying the same ranking rather than a new one, and the catch-all is still last. */}
      <div class="sb-fork">
        <button type="button" class="sb-choice" data-choose="ticket">
          <span class="sb-choice-icon sb-choice-icon-private"><Icon name="bug" size={20} /></span>
          <span class="sb-choice-title">Something is broken</span>
          <span class="sb-choice-body">
            A backup failed, a connection will not reconnect, something is not behaving. This goes
            <strong>privately</strong> to support.
          </span>
        </button>

        <button type="button" class="sb-choice" data-choose="request">
          <span class="sb-choice-icon sb-choice-icon-public"><Icon name="lightbulb" size={20} /></span>
          <span class="sb-choice-title">Something I wish existed</span>
          <span class="sb-choice-body">
            A feature, a change, an idea. This goes on the <strong>public board</strong> where other
            people can vote for it.
          </span>
        </button>

        {/* TRIMMED BY ONE CLAUSE WHEN THE COLUMN NARROWED, and by measurement rather than taste.
            The dropped clause was "or a question about what you are paying for", written for a
            344px tile; in a 224px one it took this body to 7 lines against its two neighbours' 5
            and made the whole bottom row 271px tall to hold one card's tail. The title already
            says "Account and billing", which is what the clause was restating. */}
        <button type="button" class="sb-choice" data-choose="billing">
          <span class="sb-choice-icon sb-choice-icon-admin"><Icon name="credit-card" size={20} /></span>
          <span class="sb-choice-title">Account and billing</span>
          <span class="sb-choice-body">
            An invoice, a plan change, or a payment that will not go through. This goes
            <strong>privately</strong> to the people who hold your account.
          </span>
        </button>

        <button type="button" class="sb-choice" data-choose="sales">
          <span class="sb-choice-icon sb-choice-icon-admin"><Icon name="handshake" size={20} /></span>
          <span class="sb-choice-title">Sales question</span>
          <span class="sb-choice-body">
            Not a customer yet? Pricing, limits, or whether we cover the platform you use. This
            goes <strong>privately</strong> to sales.
          </span>
        </button>

        <button type="button" class="sb-choice" data-choose="other">
          <span class="sb-choice-icon sb-choice-icon-admin">
            <Icon name="message-circle-question-mark" size={20} />
          </span>
          <span class="sb-choice-title">Something else</span>
          <span class="sb-choice-body">
            Anything that does not fit the other four. It goes <strong>privately</strong> to
            support and someone will place it for you.
          </span>
        </button>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* ── Step 2a · the three private forms ──
        One block, three kinds. The order of the fields is the order of the questions a human asks:
        who are you, where do I reply, what is this about, what happened, show me. Attachments come
        last because they are the only optional thing here, and an optional control above a required
        one is read as required.

        THE SPACE PICKER SITS UNDER THE BODY FOR THAT SAME RULE, not beside the platform field where
        it reads as belonging. It is optional, and this form's own law is that an optional control
        above a required one is read as required — putting it first, where "What is this about?"
        already lives, would put the only skippable question at the top of a form of compulsory ones.
        Under the body it also lands where it is most useful: the hint above it has just asked for run
        IDs and base names, and the picker is the structured half of the same answer.

        ITS RESTING STATE IS AN ANSWER, NOT A BLANK. `Not about a particular Space` is the first
        option and it is selected, so the control at rest says something true rather than sitting
        empty and reading as a question nobody answered. Nothing about it can block a submit: the
        validator walks `[required]`, this carries none, and the empty value is the submitted one.

        IT IS SIGNED-IN ONLY, and the whole argument for that is on `SPACE_PROSE_OUT` in the
        frontmatter. Both halves ship in the HTML — this app is a static build, so a query parameter
        cannot change what the server sent — and `html[data-portal-session]` picks one, exactly as
        the sign-in line at the foot does. `lib/submit.ts` DISABLES the select in the signed-out
        state as well as hiding it, because a control that is merely invisible is still in
        `new FormData(form)`.

        NO ASTRO COMMENT MAY GO INSIDE THE EXPRESSION BELOW. A brace-slash-star comment inside a
        template expression is an SSR 500 with an empty body that every gate passes (six occurrences
        on record), so everything worth saying about this block is said out here.

        AND THIS COMMENT MUST NEVER SPELL THE CLOSING SEQUENCE IN ITS PROSE. The first draft of this
        note quoted the very token it was warning about; the quote terminated the comment two lines
        early and the remaining sentences rendered as visible text on the page, under a build that
        was green on typecheck, build and smoke. Only reading the page caught it. */}
    {PRIVATE.map((f) => (
      <section class="sb-step" data-step={f.kind} hidden>
        <p class="sb-back">
          <button type="button" data-restart><Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back</button>
        </p>
        <h2 class="sb-h">{f.heading}</h2>
        <p class="sb-note sb-note-private">
          <Icon name="lock" size={16} />
          <span>{f.note}</span>
        </p>

        <form class="sb-form" data-form={f.kind} novalidate>
          {/* ONLY ON THE FAULT REPORT. A broken backup always happened on a platform, and knowing
              which one before anybody reads the ticket is the difference between routing it and
              guessing. A billing question and a catch-all are not about a platform at all, and
              asking there would be a required-looking control with no right answer. */}
          {f.kind === 'ticket' && <RelatedToField kind="ticket" />}

          <label>
            <span>Your name</span>
            <input type="text" name="name" required autocomplete="name" />
          </label>
          <label>
            <span>Email</span>
            <input type="email" name="email" required autocomplete="email" />
            <small>We reply here, and use it to find your account if you have one.</small>
          </label>
          <label>
            <span>Subject</span>
            <input type="text" name="subject" required data-arts-input />
          </label>

          <div class="sb-arts" data-arts hidden>
            <div class="sb-arts-top">
              <p class="sb-arts-h" data-arts-h></p>
              <button type="button" class="sb-arts-x" data-arts-dismiss>
                <Icon name="x" size={14} /> Dismiss
              </button>
            </div>
            <div class="sb-arts-list" data-arts-list></div>
            <p class="sb-arts-foot">
              None of these? Carry on below — nothing here stops you sending it.
            </p>
          </div>

          <label>
            <span>{f.bodyLabel}</span>
            <textarea name="body" rows="6" required></textarea>
            <small>
              {f.bodyHint}{' '}
              {f.space && <span data-when-signed-out>{SPACE_PROSE_OUT}</span>}
            </small>
          </label>

          {f.space && SPACE_GROUPS.length > 0 && (
            <label class="sb-space" data-space-field>
              <span>Which Space is this about?</span>
              <select name="spaceId" data-space>
                <option value="">Not about a particular Space</option>
                {SPACE_GROUPS.map((g) => (
                  <optgroup label={g.orgName}>
                    {g.spaces.map((sp) => (
                      <option value={sp.id}>{sp.name}</option>
                    ))}
                  </optgroup>
                ))}
              </select>
              <small>Optional. Picking one saves us asking which backup you mean.</small>
            </label>
          )}

          {f.kind === 'ticket' && (
            <div class="sb-hand" data-hand hidden>
              <div class="sb-hand-top">
                <button
                  type="button"
                  class="sb-hand-toggle"
                  data-hand-toggle
                  aria-expanded="false"
                  aria-controls="sb-hand-body"
                >
                  <Icon name="chevron-right" size={14} class="sb-hand-chev" />
                  <span>From your chat</span>
                  <span class="sb-hand-n" data-hand-n></span>
                </button>
                <button type="button" class="sb-hand-x" data-hand-remove>
                  <Icon name="x" size={14} /> Remove
                </button>
              </div>
              <p class="sb-hand-sum">
                What the assistant tried, the pages it cited, and the page you were reading. It is
                attached unless you remove it.
              </p>
              <div class="sb-hand-body" id="sb-hand-body" data-hand-body hidden>
                <label class="sb-hand-field">
                  <span>Edit out anything you would rather not send</span>
                  <textarea name="chat" rows="9" data-hand-text></textarea>
                </label>
              </div>
              <p class="sb-hand-wait">
                A person reads this and replies by email to the address above. We do not publish
                response times — you get an email when there is an update.
              </p>
            </div>
          )}

          <div class="sb-files" data-files>
            <span class="sb-files-h">Attachments, if they help</span>
            {/* TWO LINES, AND THE ORDER IS THE POINT. The first says what is worth attaching and
                differs per door: a screenshot of a failed run is useful to support, an invoice is
                useful to billing, and neither is useful to the other. The second is the file rule,
                derived from the same constants the validator enforces so the stated cap cannot
                drift from the enforced one. Saying only the second told a visitor what the picker
                would accept and nothing about why they might open it. */}
            <p class="sb-files-hint">{f.filesHint}</p>
            <p class="sb-files-rule" id={`sb-files-hint-${f.kind}`}>{ATTACH_HINT}</p>
            <input
              type="file"
              name="files"
              multiple
              accept={ATTACH_ACCEPT}
              data-file-input
              hidden
            />
            <button
              type="button"
              class="sb-file-add"
              data-file-add
              aria-describedby={`sb-files-hint-${f.kind}`}
            >
              <Icon name="plus" size={14} /> Choose files
            </button>
            <ul class="sb-file-list" data-file-list hidden></ul>
            <div class="sb-file-live" role="status" aria-live="polite">
              <p class="sb-file-note" data-file-note hidden></p>
              <p class="sb-file-count" data-file-count></p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p class="sb-err" data-err hidden></p>
          <button type="submit" class="sb-submit"><Icon name="send" size={15} /> {f.submit}</button>
        </form>
      </section>
    ))}

    {/* ── Step 2b · the public request ── */}
    <section class="sb-step" data-step="request" hidden>
      <p class="sb-back">
        <button type="button" data-restart><Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back</button>
      </p>
      <h2 class="sb-h">Something I wish existed</h2>
      <p class="sb-note sb-note-public">
        <Icon name="globe" size={16} />
        <span>
          <strong>This will be visible to everyone.</strong> Your request and the name you give
          appear on the public board. Your email never does.
        </span>
      </p>

      <form class="sb-form" data-form="request" novalidate>
        {/* FIRST, BECAUSE IT SCOPES EVERYTHING UNDER IT. A reader who has come from the board's
            "Suggest a platform" tile has already answered this question by clicking, and arrives
            with it filled in — see `?about=` in `lib/submit.ts`. */}
        <RelatedToField kind="request" allowNew />

        <label>
          <span>What do you want?</span>
          <input type="text" name="title" required data-dupe-input placeholder="One line, as you would say it" />
        </label>

        {/* Filled by `lib/submit.ts` from the same Pagefind index the docs search uses. */}
        <div class="sb-dupes" data-dupes hidden>
          <p class="sb-dupes-h" data-dupes-h></p>
          <div data-dupes-list></div>
        </div>

        <label>
          <span>Why — what would this let you do?</span>
          <textarea name="body" rows="5" required></textarea>
          <small>The reason is worth more than the vote. One concrete situation beats a paragraph
            of description.</small>
        </label>
        <label>
          <span>Name to show on the board</span>
          <input type="text" name="name" required autocomplete="name" placeholder="First name and initial is plenty" />
        </label>
        <label>
          <span>Email</span>
          <input type="email" name="email" required autocomplete="email" />
          <small>Never published. It counts you once and lets us tell you when this moves.</small>
        </label>
        <p class="sb-err" data-err hidden></p>
